----------- Arrays--------------------

##  What is an Array

###  Definition

An array is a collection of elements of the same data type, stored in contiguous memory locations.

###  When to use

* When you need to store multiple values of the same type
* Example: marks, numbers, names


## Memory Allocation (Heap vs Stack)

### How Arrays are Stored

* Array object → stored in Heap memory
* Reference variable → stored in Stack memory

###  Explanation

When you create an array:
int[] arr = new int[5];

* `arr` → reference variable (stored in stack)
* `new int[5]` → actual array object (stored in heap)

The stack variable `arr` holds the address/reference of the array in heap.


### Visual Representation


Stack Memory            Heap Memory
-------------          -------------------
arr  -----------►      [0, 0, 0, 0, 0]




###  Key Points

* Heap stores actual data (array elements)
* Stack stores reference to that data
* Multiple references can point to the same array
* Arrays are objects in Java → always stored in heap



### Example
int[] a = {1, 2, 3};
int[] b = a;

b[0] = 100;

System.out.println(a[0]); // Output: 100

Both `a` and `b` point to the same array in heap


##  Declaration of Array

###  Syntax
dataType[] arrayName;


###  Example
int[] arr;

##  Initialization of Array

###  Method 1: With size
int[] arr = new int[5];

###  Method 2: With values
int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};


##  Accessing Elements

arrayName[index];
### Example
System.out.println(arr[0]); // first element


##  Updating Elements
arr[1] = 100;


##  Looping through Array

###  Using for loop
for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    System.out.println(arr[i]);
}

##  for-each Loop (Enhanced for loop)
Simplified way to iterate over arrays.

###  Syntax


for (dataType variable : arrayName) {
    // sout(variable)
}


###  Example
for (int num : arr) {
    System.out.println(num);
}



##  2D Array
Array of arrays (matrix form).

###  Syntax
int[][] matrix = new int[3][3];

###  Example
int[][] matrix = {
    {1, 2, 3},
    {4, 5, 6},
    {7, 8, 9}
};


##  Traversing 2D Array
for (int i = 0; i < matrix.length; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < matrix[i].length; j++) {
        System.out.print(matrix[i][j] + " ");
    }
    System.out.println();
}

##  Jagged Array
An array where each row can have different column sizes.

###  Syntax
int[][] jagged = new int[3][];


###  Example
jagged[0] = new int[2];
jagged[1] = new int[3];
jagged[2] = new int[1];


##  Important Points

* Array index starts from 0
* Size is fixed after initialization
* `length` gives size of array
* Default values:

  * int → 0
  * boolean → false
  * object → null



